<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1740 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Rule, Britannia!</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A British song of 1740 from a play about King Alfred, urging Britain to rule the seas. It is sung every year at the Last Night of the Proms.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>James Thomson</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Thomas Arne</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>British; associated above all with the Royal Navy</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Cliveden, England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1740 (first performed 1 August 1740); first heard in London 1745</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by James Thomson, music by Thomas Arne (1740))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a>&quot; began as the final number of Alfred, a masque about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> with music by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-arne">Thomas Arne</a> and words by James Thomson and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Mallet. It was first performed on 1 August 1740 at Cliveden, the country home of Frederick, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, to mark the accession of his grandfather George I and the birthday of Princess Augusta. The song soon took on a life of its own. First heard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1745, it was at once popular, and Handel quoted its opening phrase in his Occasional Oratorio the following year. The Jacobites took it up with words altered in their favour. In 1751 Mallet reused its text, with stanzas by Lord Bolingbroke, as the chorus of the comic song &quot;Married to a Mermaid&quot;, which became very popular in his masque Britannia at Drury Lane in 1755.</p><p>The historian Armitage sees the song as the most lasting expression of the idea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> that emerged in the 1730s, built on &quot;a mixture of adulterated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a>, nationalistic anxiety and libertarian fervour&quot;. He links it with Bolingbroke's On the Idea of a Patriot King (1738), written for the same circle around Frederick: a strong navy would let an island nation do without a standing army, which since Cromwell's time had been feared as a tool of tyranny, so that naval power could be equated with civil liberty. When the song appeared it was an exhortation, not a description. Britain did not yet rule the seas; written during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear">War of Jenkins' Ear</a>, it may have answered alleged Spanish attacks on British merchant ships. Its words took on a material and patriotic sense only by the end of the 19th century. Maurice Willson Disher notes that the Victorians, who by then did command the seas, changed the refrain from an exhortation to a statement, and Richard Dawkins used that altered refrain as an example of a successful meme.</p><p>The song is most closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, but the British Army uses it too. In 1945 a massed band of Australian, British and American forces played it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Lord Mountbatten</a>, Supreme Allied Commander, arrived for the surrender of the Japanese forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>. It closes Henry Wood's Fantasia on British Sea <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> (1905) and is sung each year at the BBC's Last Night of the Proms, for many years in Malcolm Sargent's arrangement, with a guest soloist; Bryn Terfel sang its third verse in Welsh in 1994 and 2008. Its place there, and that of other patriotic songs, has been much criticised, notably by the conductor Leonard Slatkin.</p><p>In 2020 the BBC announced that &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a>&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-hope-and-glory">Land of Hope and Glory</a>&quot; would not be sung at the Last Night of the Proms. Reports linked the decision to the Black Lives Matter movement, whose supporters, after the murder of George Floyd that summer, accused the songs of glorifying colonialism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Prime Minister Boris Johnson and others protested; the BBC said the change had been due to social distancing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covid-19-pandemic">COVID-19 pandemic</a>, reversed it, and both songs were performed with a reduced choir and orchestra. A recording with the mezzo-soprano Della Jones reached number 10 in the UK Singles Chart during those Proms.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic ode urging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to rule the seas, which ties command of the sea to the liberty of the British people, set against servitude. The mood is proud and rousing, a call to naval greatness written before that greatness existed.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When Britain first, at Heaven's command</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Arose from out the azure main;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>This was the charter of the land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And guardian angels sung this strain:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Rule, Britannia! rule the waves:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Britons never will be slaves.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The nations, not so blest as thee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Must, in their turns, to tyrants fall;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>While thou shalt flourish great and free,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The dread and envy of them all.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Rule, Britannia! rule the waves:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Britons never will be slaves.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Still more majestic shalt thou rise,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>More dreadful, from each foreign stroke;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As the loud blast that tears the skies,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Serves but to root thy native oak.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Rule, Britannia! rule the waves:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Britons never will be slaves.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thee haughty tyrants ne'er shall tame:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All their attempts to bend thee down,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Will but arouse thy generous flame;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But work their woe, and thy renown.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Rule, Britannia! rule the waves:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Britons never will be slaves.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To thee belongs the rural reign;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thy cities shall with commerce shine:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All thine shall be the subject main,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And every shore it circles thine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Rule, Britannia! rule the waves:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Britons never will be slaves.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The Muses, still with freedom found,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shall to thy happy coast repair;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Blest Isle! With matchless beauty crown'd,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And manly hearts to guard the fair.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Rule, Britannia! rule the waves:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Britons never will be slaves.&quot;</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: James Thomson (1740). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rule%2C_Britannia!" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="E53mX2SwsG4" data-title="Rule, Britannia!: at the BBC Proms"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">at the BBC Proms</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel BBC Music. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E53mX2SwsG4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Occasional Oratorio (Handel)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1746</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Married to a Mermaid (David Mallet)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1751</span><span class="sg-chain-h">text reused as chorus</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variations on &quot;Rule Britannia&quot;, WoO 79 (Beethoven)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1803</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Piano Sonata No. 24, Op. 78 (Beethoven)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1809</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Wellington's Victory, Op. 91 (Beethoven)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1813</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Rise Columbia!</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Combat naval (Daniel Steibelt)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Concert overture in D major, WWV 42 (Wagner)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1837</span><span class="sg-chain-h">based on the theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Huldigung der Königin Victoria von Grossbritannien, Op. 103 (Johann Strauss I)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1838</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variations on Rule Britannia, Op. 116 (Ferdinand Ries)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fantaisie sur deux mélodies anglaises, Op. 43 (Alexandre Guilmant)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Zoo (Arthur Sullivan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1875</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">HMS Pinafore, jubilee finale (Arthur Sullivan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1887</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Utopia, Limited (Arthur Sullivan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1893</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Victoria and Merrie England (Arthur Sullivan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1897</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fantasia on British Sea Songs (Henry Wood)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1905</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as (closing number)</span></li></ul></section>
